L103: Die Tabula Peutingeriana
L106: Forschung
L108: 21.11.2018
L114: This UNESCO World Documentary Heritage object is the medieval copy of an ancient map. Originally recorded on a seven-metre parchment roll, it shows the Roman road network.
L116: Author: Michael Rathmann
L117: The Tabula Peutingeriana, UNESCO World Documentary Heritage since 2007, is a unique witness to cartographic history. The extant copy, probably made around 1200 CE, copies a late antique original.
L118: The map came into the possession of Augsburg humanist Konrad Peutinger, who made it known in scholarly circles and became its namesake; later it passed through intermediaries and reached Prince Eugene of Savoy’s collection in 1717.
L119: After Prince Eugene’s death, it entered the Vienna Court Library in 1738; it remains in the Austrian National Library as Codex Vindobonensis 324.
L120: The parchment roll consists of eleven sheets, nearly 6.80 metres long and 33 centimetres tall; it depicts the oikoumene from Spain to India in extreme distortion.
L121: The beginning with the Atlantic, parts of Iberia, West Africa and likely introductory information is missing.
L122: The map belongs to a tradition of non-scale maps outside elite mathematical-physical geography.
L126-L127: Scholarly debates concern date, sources, design, geographic accuracy, usability, purpose and medieval modifications.
L131-L134: A research project studies about 3,600 place names. Researchers interpret the object as a changing cultural representation of geographic, social, political and religious space and as a knowledge and memory medium.
L135: Author biography: Prof. Dr. Michael Rathmann, Professor of Ancient History, University of Eichstätt-Ingolstadt.
L137-L155: Full bibliography printed in the source response.

